Rule 13 now compares the entire vector, as the amended design requires, and all three lanes are green. Nothing is staged or committed, as instructed.

**How rule 13 works now** (`code.cljc`, function `canonical-projection`):
1. It first compares register ids body by body, as before. A difference is reported as `:noncanonical-registers` at the first differing pc.
2. If the ids match, it rebuilds the whole segment from the parse trees under §3.3 and compares it to the original. That means main sequence first, bodies in first-in-first-out queue order, labels placed by the walk and register numbering repeated. Any remaining difference is reported as `:noncanonical-layout` at the first differing pc.

**New refusal rows** (`code_test.cljc`; every golden is still accepted):
- **Two bodies swapped:** `(f (fn [x] x) (fn [y] y))` with its two bodies swapped and the body-pcs adjusted is refused as `:noncanonical-layout` at pc 1.
- **Nested body out of queue order:** the `:body-queue-order` program, with the body found inside another body moved ahead of a body queued earlier, is refused as `:noncanonical-layout` at pc 1.

**Label-placement variant:** my parse can't produce one where the ids still match. It uses the labels to decide where each branch of a conditional starts and ends, so moving a label makes it read a different program. The new test `moved-labels-re-parse-as-another-tree` shows `(stream-cursor (if c a b))` with its end label moved past the cursor. It parses as `(if c a (stream-cursor b))` and is refused earlier, by rule 8 (`:definite-assignment`) at pc 6.

